      the copyright owner that is granting the License.

      "Legal Entity" shall mean the union of the acting entity and all
      other entities that control, are controlled by, or are under common
      control with that entity. For the purposes of this definition,
      "control" means (i) the power, direct or indirect, to cause the
      direction or management of such entity, whether by contract or
      otherwise, or (ii) ownership of fifty percent (50%) or more of the
      outstanding shares, or (iii) beneficial ownership of such entity.

      "You" (or "Your") shall mean an individual or Legal Entity
      exercising permissions granted by this License.

      "Source" form shall mean the preferred form for making modifications,
      including but not limited to software source code, documentation
      source, and configuration files.

          Illustration: A C source file "parser.c", a Markdown file from
          which HTML documentation is generated, and a YAML configuration
          file are each in Source form.

      "Object" form shall mean any form resulting from mechanical
      transformation or translation of a Source form, including but
      not limited to compiled object code, generated documentation,
      and conversions to other media types. Object form includes,
      without limitation, the following:

          (i)   "Executable" form shall mean any Object form that is
                intended to be run or executed by a machine, whether
                directly or through an interpreter, virtual machine, or
                other runtime environment, including but not limited to
                machine code, compiled binaries, bytecode, and compiled
                libraries or modules.

          Illustrations:

          (1) A compiled program ("app.exe" or an ELF binary), a shared
              library (".so" or ".dll"), a Java ".jar" file containing
              bytecode, a Python ".pyc" file, and a minified JavaScript
              bundle are each in Executable form, and therefore also in
              Object form.

          (2) HTML pages or a PDF manual generated from documentation
              source are in Object form but not in Executable form.

      "Work" shall mean the work of authorship, whether in Source or
      Object form, made available under the License, as indicated by a
      copyright notice that is included in or attached to the work
      (an example is provided in the Appendix below).

      "Derivative Works" shall mean any file, whether in Source or Object
      form, that is based on (or derived from) a file constituting the
      Work, and in which editorial revisions, annotations, elaborations,
      additions, deletions, or other modifications to the contents of
      such file represent, as a whole, an original work of authorship.
      A file of the Work whose modifications do not meet this standard
      remains part of the Work. Derivative Works shall also include any
      new file containing copyrightable material copied or adapted from
      the Work or its Derivative Works, to the extent that such material
      is protected by copyright.

      For the purposes of this License, Derivative Works shall be
      determined on a file-by-file basis and shall not include separate
      files that do not incorporate copyrightable material from the Work
      or its Derivative Works, including files that merely link to, call,
      or reference their interfaces without incorporating copyrightable
      material from them.

          Illustrations:

          (1) You fix a bug in "parser.c", a file of the Work, by
              rewriting part of a function. Your modified "parser.c" is
              a Derivative Work. If You only correct a spelling error in
              a comment, Your change is not an original work of
              authorship, and the modified "parser.c" remains part of
              the Work. Either way, it stays under this License whenever
              its Source form is shared.

          (2) You copy a function from "parser.c" into a new file,
              "fastparse.c". "fastparse.c" is a Derivative Work, to the
              extent the copied function is protected by copyright.

          (3) You write a new file, "app.c", that includes the Work's
              header "parser.h" and calls the functions declared in it,
              but copies no code from the Work. "app.c" is not a
              Derivative Work.

          (4) You write a new file, "plugin.py", that imports a module of
              the Work and extends its classes without copying their
              code. "plugin.py" is not a Derivative Work.

      "Contribution" shall mean any work of authorship, including
      the original version of the Work and any modifications or additions
      to that Work or Derivative Works thereof, that is intentionally
      submitted to Licensor for inclusion in the Work by the copyright
      owner or by an individual or Legal Entity authorized to submit on
      behalf of the copyright owner. For the purposes of this definition,
      "submitted" means any form of electronic, verbal, or written
      communication sent to the Licensor or its representatives, including
      but not limited to communication on electronic mailing lists, source
      code control systems, and issue tracking systems that are managed
      by, or on behalf of, the Licensor for the purpose of discussing and
      improving the Work, but excluding communication that is
      conspicuously marked or otherwise designated in writing by the
      copyright owner as "Not a Contribution."

          Illustration: A pull request submitted to the Work's official
          repository is a Contribution. A patch that its author posts to
          the project's mailing list marked "Not a Contribution" is not.

      "Contributor" shall mean Licensor and any individual or Legal Entity
      on behalf of whom a Contribution has been received by Licensor and
      subsequently incorporated within the Work.

      "Larger Work" shall mean any work, whether in Source or Object form,
      that combines the Work or its Derivative Works with other material
      contained in a separate file or files that do not constitute
      Derivative Works. Such separate files shall not become Derivative
      Works merely by being combined, compiled, linked, packaged, or
      distributed together with the Work or its Derivative Works as part
      of a Larger Work.

          Illustration: You build a product from the Work's "parser.c",
          your own files "app.c" and "ui.c", and a third-party library,
          and ship it as one installer or one compiled program. The
          product is a Larger Work. "parser.c", whether modified or not,
          remains under this License whenever its Source form is shared.
          "app.c", "ui.c", the third-party library, and the product as a
          whole may be under any terms You choose.

      The Illustrations in this License are examples only. They do not
